From ad694804e1b373dac29e1df6eba777c730833710 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Mon, 13 Jul 2026 10:44:23 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] patch 0275 -- #1 FIX (real root): null-guard game.lang access
 macros (rtl + _())

THE fix for the organya cold-cache precache crash. Patch 0272's in-LOG fault
handler named it decisively, REFUTING the OOM hypothesis: a SIGSEGV null deref,
not bad_alloc. eip=I18N::isRTL()+0x7 <- Font::getWidth+0x23 <-
_oc_draw_progress+0x1a0 (heap fine: uordblks ~3.36MB, 250-850KB free).

Root: the rtl() macro (game.h) = `game.lang->isRTL()` with no null guard, and
game.lang (I18N*) is new'd at game.cpp:99 -- AFTER the first-launch organya
precache. So the precache progress-overlay's text measure (_oc_draw_progress ->
Font::getWidth -> rtl()) dereferences a NULL game.lang. Benign on DOSBox (null
page readable -> precache completes) but a hard SIGSEGV on g2k/CWSDPMI -- and
since it fires in VESA graphics mode the DJGPP register dump lands on the unshown
text buffer, hence the operator's "silent quit-to-DOS" across 4 iters. (0273's
exception guard can't catch a signal, so it was ineffective; 0274 moot -- both
dropped.)

Fix: null-guard BOTH game.lang-access macros to close the whole class of pre-I18N
null derefs:
  rtl() -> (game.lang ? game.lang->isRTL() : false)   [LTR default]
  _()   -> inline _i18n_tr() returning game.lang ? translate(key) : key
Note: _() uses an inline helper, NOT a ternary macro -- translate() returns
`const std::string&`, so a `game.lang ? translate(x) : x` ternary would collapse
to a by-VALUE copy for string-literal keys (perf + dangling hazard); the helper
preserves the const-ref return. Its null branch returns the key ref (valid for
the immediate-use pattern _() is always used in). LTR / raw-key defaults are
correct pre-I18N (overlay text is literal English); zero change once game.lang is
set. Keep 0272 riding for the re-verify (organya cold-cache now boots THROUGH).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/game.h | 28 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/game.h b/src/game.h
index 1f03da4d..8f25e3e7 100644
--- a/src/game.h
+++ b/src/game.h
@@ -190,8 +190,32 @@ struct Game
 #define NXFLAG_SLOW_WHEN_HURT (NXFLAG_SLOW_X_WHEN_HURT | NXFLAG_SLOW_Y_WHEN_HURT)
 
 extern Game game;
-#define _(x) game.lang->translate(x)
-#define rtl() game.lang->isRTL()
+// DOS-PORT (patch 0275, #1 FIX): null-guard the game.lang access macros.
+// game.lang (I18N*, above) is new'd at game.cpp:99 -- AFTER the organya
+// first-launch precache runs. The precache progress-overlay text
+// (_oc_draw_progress -> Font::getWidth/draw -> rtl()) calls rtl() while game.lang
+// is NULL. On g2k/CWSDPMI that null `this` deref is a hard SIGSEGV (confirmed by
+// patch 0272's fault handler: eip=I18N::isRTL()+0x7 <- Font::getWidth+0x23 <-
+// _oc_draw_progress+0x1a0) -- benign on DOSBox (null page readable), fatal on
+// real HW, and invisible (the DJGPP dump lands on the unshown VESA text buffer).
+// This closes the whole CLASS of pre-I18N game.lang-null derefs, not just the
+// one instance. LTR / raw-key defaults are correct pre-I18N (the overlay text is
+// literal English); zero behavior change once game.lang is set.
+//
+// _() uses an inline helper (NOT a `game.lang ? translate(x) : x` ternary) on
+// purpose: translate() returns `const std::string&`, so the ternary with a
+// string-literal key would collapse to a by-VALUE std::string (an extra copy per
+// call + a dangling-ref hazard). The helper preserves the const-ref return. Its
+// null branch returns the key ref, valid for the immediate-use pattern _() is
+// always used in (font.draw(_("..."))); a `const std::string& s = _(x)` binding
+// while game.lang is NULL (early boot only) would be the sole caveat, and no
+// early code does that.
+inline const std::string &_i18n_tr(const std::string &key)
+{
+  return game.lang ? game.lang->translate(key) : key;
+}
+#define _(x) _i18n_tr(x)
+#define rtl() (game.lang ? game.lang->isRTL() : false)
 extern TextBox textbox;
 
 extern Object *onscreen_objects[MAX_OBJECTS];
